"**Worked example**\n1. **First attempt** \u2013 The worker calls an external payment API to charge $10 and includes an idempotency key `order\u2011123`. The API processes the charge and returns success. The worker then writes `order\u2011123 \u2192 success` to its local outbox table and finally sends an acknowledgement to the message broker.\n2. **Ack lost** \u2013 The acknowledgement never reaches the broker (network glitch or process crash). The broker assumes the message was not processed and re\u2011queues it for delivery.\n3. **Retry** \u2013 The worker is invoked again with the same message. It again calls the payment API with the same idempotency key `order\u2011123`. Because the key was recorded **atomically with the external effect** on the API side, the API recognises the duplicate request and returns the original result without charging the customer again.\n4. **Crash window** \u2013 Suppose the worker crashes **after** the API reports success but **before** it records the key in the outbox. On retry, the worker calls the API with `order\u2011123`. The API has no record of the key yet, so it processes the charge a second time, causing a duplicate external effect. Only after the second call does the worker finally write the key, but the duplicate charge has already occurred.\n**Key take\u2011aways**: a retry can duplicate a successful external effect if the idempotency key isn\u2019t persisted atomically with the effect. The receiver (the external API) must enforce idempotency using a stable key, and the sender must ensure the key is recorded before acknowledging the message to close the failure window."
